<p>Because Bexar County can see dozens of hail reports in an active year, insurers often ask which storm caused the damage. Dating the loss correctly is one of the first things we work on.</p>

<h2>What Hail Damages on San Antonio Properties</h2>
<p>Hail is a named peril on most homeowners and commercial property policies, subject to deductibles, exclusions, and endorsements. Claims here frequently involve:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Roofs.</strong> Composition shingles (granule loss, bruising, fractured mats), standing-seam metal common toward the Hill Country edge, tile, and flat or low-slope commercial membranes. See our <a href="/services/roof-damage-insurance-claims/">roof damage insurance claims</a> page.</li>
<li><strong>Windows and skylights.</strong> Broken glass, split frames, torn screens.</li>
<li><strong>Exterior walls.</strong> Spatter and chips on stucco, cracked siding, damaged trim and fascia. Limestone and brick veneer usually hold up, but mortar, sealants, and trim around them may not.</li>
<li><strong>Soft metals and equipment.</strong> Gutters, downspouts, roof vents, and condenser coil fins on AC units.</li>
<li><strong>Other structures.</strong> Fences, carports, sheds, pergolas, and metal outbuildings.</li>
<li><strong>Interior water damage</strong> from broken windows or punctured roofing, which is usually handled as part of the same hail claim.</li>
</ul>

