<h2>Common Insurer Arguments on Hail Claims</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>&ldquo;Wear and tear, not hail.&rdquo;</strong> The insurer attributes granule loss or cracking to age, blistering, or manufacturing defects. South Texas heat ages shingles, which gives this argument traction.</li>
<li><strong>&ldquo;Prior storm.&rdquo;</strong> The carrier says the damage came from an earlier, unreported storm or one before the policy period.</li>
<li><strong>&ldquo;Cosmetic only.&rdquo;</strong> Dents on metal roofing or soft metals are labeled cosmetic under an exclusion endorsement.</li>
<li><strong>Repair instead of replace.</strong> The estimate allows spot repairs even when brittle or discontinued materials cannot be repaired or matched.</li>
<li><strong>Missing line items.</strong> Drip edge, starter course, ridge, flashing, permits, and code-required items are left out. Our <a href="/services/insurance-estimate-review/">insurance estimate review</a> looks for these gaps.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Settlement Factors to Understand</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>Wind/hail deductibles.</strong> Often a percentage of the dwelling limit. Check your declarations page.</li>
<li><strong>ACV vs. replacement cost.</strong> Under replacement cost coverage, insurers typically pay actual cash value first and release recoverable depreciation after repairs are finished. Some roof coverage is ACV-only or follows a payment schedule. See <a href="/replacement-cost-vs-actual-cash-value-texas/">replacement cost vs. actual cash value in Texas</a>.</li>
<li><strong>Code upgrades.</strong> Re-roofing inside city limits generally requires a permit through the City of San Antonio Development Services Department. Code-required work is paid only if your policy includes ordinance or law coverage, up to its limit. See <a href="/local-building-codes/">local building codes</a>.</li>
</ul>
<p>Personal property, other structures, and additional living expenses each have their own limits, so check every coverage section, not only the dwelling limit.</p>

<h2>How San Antonio Public Adjuster Can Help</h2>
<p>San Antonio Public Adjuster is a public adjusting service of Rise Public Adjusting LLC, licensed by the Texas Department of Insurance. A licensed public adjuster represents the policyholder, not the insurer. On a hail claim, that may include inspecting the roof and exterior, reviewing the policy, preparing a detailed estimate, gathering weather data, and communicating with the insurer's adjuster. If the claim was already paid low, we can review it for an <a href="/services/insurance-claim-supplements/">insurance claim supplement</a> or discuss whether <a href="/services/insurance-appraisal/">appraisal</a> fits a dispute over the amount of loss. We cannot guarantee a result. Fees are disclosed in a written contract before any work begins; Texas law caps public adjuster compensation at 10% of the insurance settlement.</p>

<h2>Your Responsibilities After a Hailstorm</h2>
<ul>
<li>Give prompt notice to your insurer.</li>
<li>Protect the property from further damage (tarp openings, board windows) and keep receipts.</li>
<li>Photograph damage before repairs and keep samples of damaged materials when practical.</li>
<li>Submit a proof of loss if requested, within the time your policy requires.</li>
<li>Cooperate with inspections and reasonable document requests.</li>
</ul>
<p>Many policies also set a contractual deadline to file suit. For legal questions about deadlines or a dispute, consult a Texas attorney.</p>
